1 EDIT

   Edits a file with an editor on the host, such as vi.  govax copies
   the file to a scratch directory on the host as lines of text, runs
   the editor on the copy, and if the editor wrote it, makes what it
   wrote the file's next version.  This command is govax's own: VMS's
   EDT and TPU aren't there.

   Format

     EDIT filespec

   SET EDITOR says which editor runs, and where its copies go.

2 Parameter

 filespec

   One file.  If no file has the name, and the name has no version, EDIT
   makes a new one (variable-length records, carriage return carriage
   control) if the editor writes it.

2 Description

   The new version has the record format, record attributes, protection,
   and owner of the version edited, so each line becomes a record of the
   same kind:

     Fixed      A short line is filled out with blanks.  The editor
                doesn't see the blanks at the end of a record.
     Variable,  The line is the record.
     stream
     VFC        The line is the record, after control bytes: those all
                the records had, or if they differed, the usual ones for
                the file, with a warning.

   A line longer than a fixed record, or than a file's maximum record
   size, loses the blanks at its end if that is enough.  Otherwise it is
   EDIT-E-TOOLONG, and there is no new version.

   The file is left alone if the editor doesn't write its copy (:q! in
   vi) or ends with a failure status (:cq in vi).  When the editor wrote
   its copy but that can't become a new version, the message ends with
   -EDIT-I-KEPT and the copy's name; the copy stays until you delete it.

   A file that isn't lines of text is refused before the editor starts:
   a directory, a relative or indexed file, one with undefined records,
   or one whose records hold a null byte or a line feed.  So is a file
   on a volume mounted read-only.

   Every process waits while the editor runs.

2 Qualifiers
/HOST

   The file is a host file, replaced when the editor has written its
   copy.

2 Examples

   1.$ EDIT LOGIN.COM

     Edits LOGIN.COM in the default directory, making a new version.

   2.$ SET EDITOR/COMMAND="vim -n %file"
     $ EDIT "notes.txt"/HOST

     Edits a host file with vim.
